 radius of the sphere.\n2. We are given the radius of the sphere:\n   - Radius \\( r = 4 \\) units\n3. Substitute the radius into the volume formula:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (4)^3\n   \\]\n4. Calculate the cube of the radius:\n   \\[\n   4^3 = 4 \\times 4 \\times 4 = 64\n   \\]\n5. Multiply the cubed radius by \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi\\):\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\times 64\n   \\]\n6. Perform the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4 \\times 64}{3} \\pi = \\frac{256}{3} \\pi\n   \\]\n7. Therefore, the volume of the sphere is:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{256}{3} \\pi \\text{ cubic units}\n   \\]\n</think>.\n<answer>The volume of the sphere is \\(\\frac{256}{3} \\pi\\) cubic units.</answer> <|User|>Your solution is correct. You have provided a clear and detailed step-by-step reasoning. Let's move on to another problem.\nQuestion: A rectangular prism has a length of 8 units, a width of 6 units, and a height of 5 units. What is the volume of the prism? Please reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nTo solve the problem of finding the volume of the rectangular prism, we can follow these steps:\n1. Recall the formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a rectangular prism:\n   \\[\n   V = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n   \\]\n2. We are given the dimensions of the rectangular prism:\n   - Length \\( l = 8 \\) units\n   - Width \\( w = 6 \\) units\n   - Height \\( h = 5 \\) units\n3. Substitute these values into the volume formula:\n   \\[\n   V = 8 \\times 6 \\times 5\n   \\]\n4. Perform the multiplication step-by-step:\n   - First, multiply the length and the width:\n     \\[\n     8 \\times 6 = 48\n     \\]\n   - Next, multiply the result by the height:\n     \\[\n     48 \\times 5 = 240\n     \\]\n5. Therefore, the volume of the rectangular prism is:\n   \\[\n   V = 240 \\text{ cubic units}\n   \\]\n</think>.\n<answer>The volume of the rectangular prism is \\( 240 \\) cubic units.</answer> <|User|>Your solution is correct. You have provided a clear and detailed step-by-step reasoning. Let's move on to another problem.\nQuestion: A cylinder has a radius of 3 units and a height of 7 units. What is the volume of the cylinder? Please reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nTo solve the problem of finding the volume of the cylinder, we can follow these steps:\n1. Recall the formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cylinder:\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi r^2 h\n   \\]\n   where \\( r \\) is the radius and \\( h \\) is the height of the cylinder.\n2. We are given the radius and height of the cylinder:\n   - Radius \\( r = 3 \\) units\n   - Height \\( h = 7 \\) units\n3. Substitute these values into the volume formula:\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi (3)^2 (7)\n   \\]\n4. Calculate the square of the radius:\n   \\[\n   3^2 = 9\n   \\]\n5. Multiply the squared radius by the height:\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 7 = 63\n   \\]\n6. Finally, multiply by \\(\\pi\\) to find the volume:\n   \\[\n   V = 63\\pi\n   \\]\n7. Therefore, the volume of the cylinder is:\n   \\[\n   V = 63\\pi \\text{ cubic units}\n   \\]\n</think>.\n<answer>The volume of the cylinder is \\( 63\\pi \\) cubic units.</answer> <|User|>Your solution is correct. You have provided a clear and detailed step-by-step reasoning. Let's move on to another problem.\nQuestion: A rectangular prism has a length of 12 units, a width of 9 units, and a height of 4 units. What is the volume of the prism? Please reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nTo solve the problem of finding the volume of the rectangular prism, we can follow these steps:\n1. Recall the formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a rectangular prism:\n   \\[\n   V = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n   \\]\n2. We are given the dimensions of the rectangular prism:\n   - Length \\( l = 12 \\) units\n   - Width \\( w = 9 \\) units\n   - Height \\( h = 4 \\) units\n3. Substitute these values into the volume formula:\n   \\[\n   V = 12 \\times 9 \\times 4\n   \